 {"title": "Watch the McMurtry Speirling Fan Car Drive Upside Down", "url": "https://www.thedrive.com/news/watch-the-mcmurtry-speirling-fan-car-drive-upside-down"}, {"title": "watch the mcmurtry speirling fan car drive upside down", "url": "https://www.thedrive.com/news/watch-the-mcmurtry-speirling-fan-car-drive-upside-down.md"}, {"title": "www.carscoops.com", "url": "https://www.carscoops.com/?p=3188676"}, {"title": "This article was published on April 14, 2025", "url": "https://thenextweb.com/news/how-the-mcmurtry-speirling-drove-upside-down"}, {"title": "WORLD FIRST: McMurtry Spéirling drives upside down from stationary", "url": "https://mcmurtry.com/upside-down-driving/"}]}, "The driver was **Thomas Yates**, co-founder and Managing Director of McMurtry Automotive. The stunt was announced around 11 April 2025.\n\n- **Driver:** Yates is described as drove forward with no support once the car was inverted, and the company's own release names him as the person who ran the demonstration (index 9-2).\n- **Setup:** Accounts differ on the apparatus. Several outlets say the car sat on a special rig which could rotate through 180 degrees, while TNW says McMurtry steered the Spéirling up a ramp and drove it upside down.\n- **Location:** The demo took place at McMurtry's headquarters in Gloucestershire, England, in front of employees and independent adjudicators.\n- **How it worked:** The car's fans create the grip. Per one source, the system generates 2000kg of downforce even at zero speed.\n- **Duration:** TNW reports the car did that for over a minute while keeping the vehicle completely stationary, apart from a brief acceleration.\n\nThe stunt was released alongside a Top Gear test track record for the same car, which is why coverage is often combined."], "durationSeconds": 7.378073457999999, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01CTPVpb6FSC7bWAVsAPZdvK", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
